response:
 To implement the Bubble Sort algorithm in C++ with minimal memory usage and error avoidance, follow these steps:

1. Allocate space on the stack for the array instead of using dynamic memory allocation.
2. Use constants or compile-time variables for array size and swap variable names.
3. Handle edge cases and potential errors with proper checks and exceptions.

Here's the code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp> // For JSON handling (optional)

using json = nlohmann::json;

constexpr int ARRAY_SIZE = 6;

void bubbleSort(int arr[ARRAY_SIZE]) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
        for (size_t j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

